.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-only
.Dd @NCOLLECTD_DATE@
.Dt NCOLLECTD-PERL 5
.Os ncollectd @NCOLLECTD_VERSION@
.Sh NAME
.Nm ncollectd-perl
.Nd Documentation of ncollectd's perl plugin
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Bd -literal -compact
\fBload-plugin\fP perl
\fBplugin\fP perl {
    \fBinclude-dir\fP \fI/path/to/perl/modules\fP
    \fBbase-name\fP \fIname\fP
    \fBenable-debugger\fP \fIpackage\fP
    \fBload-plugin\fP \fIModule::Name\fP
    \fBplugin\fP \fIname\fP {
        ...
    }
}
.Ed
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The \fBperl\fP plugin embeds a Perl interpreter into ncollectd and provides an
interface to ncollectd's plugin system.
This makes it possible to write plugins for ncollectd in Perl.
.Pp
The interface is the \fBNCollectd\fP module, which has the same object model as
the \fBncollectd\fP module of the python plugin, see
.Xr ncollectd-python 5 .
The module is built into the plugin: it is implemented in C and it does not
need any Perl module, there is no \f(CWNCollectd.pm\fP file.
.Pp
Available configuration options:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It \fBinclude-dir\fP \fI/path/to/perl/modules\fP
Prepends \fI/path/to/perl/modules\fP to the \f(CW@INC\fP array.
This is the same as using the \f(CW-I\fP command line option
or \f(CWuse lib\fP in the source code.
Please note that it only has effect on modules loaded after this option.
You can use multiple \fBinclude-dir\fP lines to add more than one directory.
.It \fBbase-name\fP \fIname\fP
Prepends \fIname\fP\fB::\fP to all the module names loaded after this option.
This is provided for convenience to keep module names short.
.It \fBenable-debugger\fP \fIpackage\fP
Run ncollectd under the control of the Perl source debugger.
If \fIpackage\fP is not the empty string, control is passed to the debugging,
profiling, or tracing module installed as Devel::\fIpackage\fP.
A comma-separated list of options may be specified after the "=" character.
Please note that you may not leave out the \fIpackage\fP option even if you
specify \fB""\fP.
This is the same as using the \f(CW-d:Package\fP command line option.
It has no effect if it is used after \fBload-plugin\fP.
.Pp
See \fBperldebug\fP for detailed documentation about debugging Perl.
.Pp
This option does not prevent ncollectd from daemonizing, so you should start
ncollectd with the \f(CW-f\fP command line option.
Else you will not be able to use the command line driven interface of the
debugger.
.It \fBload-plugin\fP \fIModule::Name\fP
Loads the Perl module \fIModule::Name\fP, this does the same as \f(CWrequire\fP
would do in a Perl program.
If your module is not found, be sure its directory is in \f(CW@INC\fP,
you can prepend directories to \f(CW@INC\fP using the \fBinclude-dir\fP
configuration option.
As a side effect, the first occurrence of this option causes the
Perl interpreter to be initialized.
.It \fBplugin\fP \fIname\fP
This block may be used to pass on configuration settings to a Perl module.
The configuration is converted into an instance of the \fBNCollectd::Config\fP
class which is passed to the registered configuration callback.
See below for details about the \fBNCollectd::Config\fP class and how to
register callbacks.
.Pp
The \fIname\fP identifies the callback.
It is used literally and independent of the \fBbase-name\fP setting.
.El
.Sh STRINGS
The strings are passed between Perl and ncollectd as bytes, and ncollectd uses
UTF-8.
The strings received from ncollectd are byte strings, use \f(CWutf8::decode\fP
if you need character strings.
The character strings passed to ncollectd must be encoded with
\f(CWutf8::encode\fP if they are not already UTF-8 encoded byte strings,
for example when they are decoded from a file or they are literals of a source
file with \f(CWuse utf8\fP.
These strings must not contain a NUL character.
.Sh WRITING YOUR OWN PLUGINS
Writing your own plugins is quite simple. ncollectd manages plugins by means of
\fBdispatch functions\fP which call the appropriate \fBcallback functions\fP
registered by the plugins.
Any plugin basically consists of the implementation of these callback functions
and initializing code which registers the functions with ncollectd.
See the section \fBEXAMPLES\fP below for a really basic example.
The following types of \fBcallback functions\fP are known to ncollectd
(all of them are optional):
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It \fBconfiguration functions\fP
These are called during configuration if an appropriate
\fBplugin\fP block has been encountered.
It is called once for each \fBplugin\fP block which matches the name of
the callback as provided with the \f(CWregister_config\fP function - see below.
.It \fBinit functions\fP
These are called once after loading the module and before any
calls to the read and write functions.
It should be used to initialize the internal state of the plugin
(e. g. open sockets, ...).
.It \fBread functions\fP
These are used to collect the actual data.
It is called once per interval (see the \fBinterval\fP configuration
option of ncollectd).
Usually it will call the \f(CWdispatch\fP method of a
\fBNCollectd::MetricFamily\fP object to dispatch the metrics to ncollectd
which will pass them on to all registered \fBwrite functions\fP.
If this function dies the plugin will be skipped for an increasing amount of
time until it returns normally again.
.It \fBwrite functions\fP
These are used to write the dispatched values.
It is called once for every metric family that was dispatched by any plugin.
.It \fBlog functions\fP
These are used to pass messages of plugins or the daemon itself to the user.
.It \fBnotification function\fP
These are used to act upon notifications.
In general, a notification is a status message that may be associated with
a data instance.
.It \fBshutdown functions\fP
These are called once before the daemon shuts down.
It should be used to clean up the plugin (e.g. close sockets, ...).
.El
.Pp
The values returned by the callback functions are ignored.
Any function (except log functions) may die in case of errors,
the error will be passed on to the user using ncollectd's logging mechanism.
If a log callback dies the error will be printed to standard error instead.
.Pp
See the documentation of the various \fBregister_\fP functions in the section
\fBFUNCTIONS\fP below for the number and types of arguments passed to each
\fBcallback function\fP.
This section also explains how to register \fBcallback functions\fP
with ncollectd.
.Pp
To enable a module, copy it to a place where Perl can find it (i. e. a
directory listed in \f(CW@INC\fP) just as any other Perl module and add
an appropriate \fBload-plugin\fP option to the configuration file.
After restarting ncollectd you're done.
.Sh CLASSES
The following classes are used to pass values between the Perl plugin
and ncollectd.
.Pp
The objects are blessed hash references, and every member of a class has an
accessor method with the same name: \f(CW$object->member\fP returns the member
and \f(CW$object->member($value)\fP checks and sets it.
The members can also be accessed directly, i.e. \f(CW$object->{member}\fP,
but then the values are only checked when they are dispatched.
The members that are hash or array references can be modified in place,
i.e. \f(CW$metric->labels->{name} = 'value'\fP.
.Pp
The objects are created with the \f(CWnew\fP class method, its arguments are
the members of the class in the documented order, as in the python plugin.
An undefined optional argument is the same as an omitted one.
.Pp
The conversion of an object to a string shows its class and members, which is
useful for debugging.
The classes can be derived in Perl with \f(CW@ISA\fP.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It \fBNCollectd::Error\fP
This is an exception.
If any Perl callback dies with an object of this class it will still be
treated like an error by ncollectd, but it will be logged as a warning instead
of an error.
.Bd -literal
    die NCollectd::Error->new("message");
.Ed
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It \fBmessage\fP
The message of the error, it is also the conversion of the object to a string.
.El
.It \fBNCollectd::Config\fP
The Config class is an object which keeps the information provided in the
configuration file.
The array of children keeps one entry for each configuration option.
Each such entry is another Config instance, which may nest further if
nested blocks are used.
.Bd -literal
    NCollectd::Config->new(key[, parent][, values][, children])
.Ed
.Pp
This represents a piece of ncollectd's config file.
It is passed to modules with config callbacks (see \f(CWregister_config\fP)
and is of little use if created somewhere else.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It \fBkey\fP
This is the keyword of this item, i.e. the first word of any given line in the
config file.
It will always be a string.
.It \fBparent\fP
This is the parent of this item, it is undefined for the root of the
configuration tree.
It is a weak reference: it is undefined if the parent is freed.
.It \fBvalues\fP
This is a reference to an array (which might be empty) of all values, i.e.
words following the keyword in any given line in the config file.
.Pp
Every item in this array will be either a string, a number or a boolean,
depending on the contents of the configuration file.
.It \fBchildren\fP
This is a reference to an array of child nodes.
For most nodes this will be empty.
If this node represents a block instead of a single line of the config file
it will contain all nodes in this block.
.El
.It \fBNCollectd::Metric\fP
This is the base class of all the metrics.
.Bd -literal
    NCollectd::Metric->new([labels][, time][, interval])
.Ed
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It \fBlabels\fP
It has to be a hash reference of numbers, strings or booleans.
The undefined values are ignored.
.It \fBtime\fP
This is the Unix timestamp of the time this metric was read.
For dispatching metrics this can be set to zero which means "now".
.It \fBinterval\fP
The interval is the timespan in seconds between two submits for the same
metric.
If this member is set to zero, the default value as specified in the config
file will be used (default: 10).
.El
