.Pp
Methods defined here:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It \fBappend\fP(\fImetric\fP | [\fImetric\fP, ...], ...)
Appends the metrics to the family, the metrics can be given in a list or in
array references.
The class of the metrics must match the type of the family.
.It \fBdispatch\fP([\fItime\fP])
Dispatches the metrics of the family to the ncollectd process, then the
metrics of the family are removed.
.El
.Pp
Members:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It \fBname\fP
The name of this metric family.
.It \fBtype\fP
The type of this metric family, it can not be modified.
Compare to \fBMETRIC_UNKNOWN\fP, \fBMETRIC_GAUGE\fP,
\fBMETRIC_COUNTER\fP, \fBMETRIC_STATE_SET\fP,
\fBMETRIC_INFO\fP, \fBMETRIC_SUMMARY\fP, \fBMETRIC_HISTOGRAM\fP
or \fBMETRIC_GAUGE_HISTOGRAM\fP.
.It \fBhelp\fP
A brief description of the metric family.
.It \fBunit\fP
The unit of the metrics.
.It \fBmetrics\fP
An array reference of the metrics.
.El
.It \fBNCollectd::Notification\fP
A notification is an object defining the severity and message of the status
message as well as an identification of a data instance.
.Bd -literal
    NCollectd::Notification->new(name[, severity][, time][, labels][, annotations])
.Ed
.Pp
The Notification class is a wrapper around the ncollectd notification.
It can be used to notify other plugins about bad stuff happening.
Notifications can be dispatched at any time and can be received with
\f(CWregister_notification\fP.
.Pp
Methods defined here:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It \fBdispatch\fP()
Dispatches the notification to the ncollectd process.
.El
.Pp
Members:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It \fBname\fP
Name of the notification.
.It \fBseverity\fP
The severity of this notification, the default is \fBNOTIF_FAILURE\fP.
Assign or compare to \fBNOTIF_FAILURE\fP, \fBNOTIF_WARNING\fP or
\fBNOTIF_OKAY\fP.
.It \fBtime\fP
The Unix timestamp of the notification, zero means "now".
.It \fBlabels\fP
A hash reference like the labels of the metrics.
.It \fBannotations\fP
A hash reference like the labels of the metrics.
.El
.El
.Sh FUNCTIONS
The following functions of the \fBNCollectd\fP package provide the interface
to ncollectd.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It \fBregister_*\fP(\fIcallback\fP[, \fIdata\fP][, \fIname\fP]) -> \fIidentifier\fP
There are seven different register functions to get callback for seven
different events.
With one exception all of them are called as shown above.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It \fIcallback\fP
Is a code reference, or the name of a subroutine, that will be called every
time the event is triggered.
If the name is not fully qualified the subroutine is looked up in the package
of the caller.
.It \fIdata\fP
Is an optional value that will be passed back to the callback function, as its
last argument, every time it is called.
If you omit this parameter, or it is undefined, no value is passed back to
your callback, not even undef.
.It \fIname\fP
Is an optional identifier for this callback.
The default name is the name of the package where the callback was defined.
Every callback needs a unique identifier, so if you want to register the same
callback multiple times in the same module you need to
specify a name here.
Otherwise it's safe to ignore this parameter.
.It \fIidentifier\fP
Is the full identifier assigned to this callback: \fBperl/\fP\fIname\fP.
.El
.Pp
These functions are called in the various stages of the daemon (see the section
\fBWRITING YOUR OWN PLUGINS\fP above) and are passed the following arguments:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It \fBregister_config\fP
The only argument passed is a \fBNCollectd::Config\fP object.
See above for the layout of this data type.
Note that you cannot receive the whole config files this way, only
\fBplugin\fP blocks inside the perl configuration block.
Additionally you will only receive blocks where the name of your callback
matches the name of the block.
.It \fBregister_init\fP
The callback will be called without arguments.
.It \fBregister_read\fP(\fIcallback\fP[, \fIinterval\fP][, \fIdata\fP][, \fIname\fP]) -> \fIidentifier\fP
This function takes an additional parameter: \fIinterval\fP.
It specifies the time in seconds between calls to the callback function.
.Pp
The callback will be called without arguments.
.It \fBregister_shutdown\fP
The callback will be called without arguments.
.It \fBregister_write\fP
The callback function will be called with one argument passed, which will be a
\fBNCollectd::MetricFamily\fP object.
For the layout of \fBNCollectd::MetricFamily\fP see above.
.It \fBregister_log\fP
The arguments are \fIseverity\fP and \fImessage\fP.
The severity is an integer and small for important messages and high for less
important messages.
The least important level is \fBLOG_DEBUG\fP, the most important level
is \fBLOG_ERROR\fP.
In between there are (from least to most important): \fBLOG_INFO\fP,
\fBLOG_NOTICE\fP, and \fBLOG_WARNING\fP.
\fImessage\fP is simply a string \fBwithout\fP a newline at the end.
.Pp
The messages logged by a log callback are not passed to the log callbacks.
If the message is logged while the Perl interpreter is used by another thread,
it is passed to the log callbacks when the interpreter is released.
.Pp
If this callback dies the error will \fBnot\fP be logged.
It will just be printed to the standard error, which usually means
silently ignored.
.It \fBregister_notification\fP
The only argument passed is a \fBNCollectd::Notification\fP object.
See above for the layout of this data type.
.El
.It \fBunregister_*\fP(\fIidentifier\fP)
Removes a callback from ncollectd's internal list of callback functions.
Every \fBregister_*\fP function has an \fBunregister_*\fP function.
\fIidentifier\fP is either the string that was returned by the register function
or a code reference.
The identifier will be constructed in the same way as for the register
functions.
.It \fBerror\fP, \fBwarning\fP, \fBnotice\fP, \fBinfo\fP, \fBdebug\fP(\fImessage\fP, ...)
Log a message with the specified severity.
The arguments are concatenated.
.El
.Sh CONSTANTS
The following constants are defined in the \fBNCollectd\fP package:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It \fBLOG_ERROR\fP, \fBLOG_WARNING\fP, \fBLOG_NOTICE\fP, \fBLOG_INFO\fP, \fBLOG_DEBUG\fP
The severities of the log messages.
.It \fBNOTIF_FAILURE\fP, \fBNOTIF_WARNING\fP, \fBNOTIF_OKAY\fP
The severities of the notifications.
.It \fBMETRIC_UNKNOWN\fP, \fBMETRIC_GAUGE\fP, \fBMETRIC_COUNTER\fP, \fBMETRIC_STATE_SET\fP, \fBMETRIC_INFO\fP, \fBMETRIC_SUMMARY\fP, \fBMETRIC_HISTOGRAM\fP, \fBMETRIC_GAUGE_HISTOGRAM\fP
The types of the metric families.
.El
.Sh EXPORTS
By default no symbols are exported, the functions and constants can be used
with their fully qualified names, i.e. \f(CWNCollectd::register_read\fP and
\f(CWNCollectd::METRIC_GAUGE\fP.
The \f(CWuse NCollectd\fP statement can import any function or constant by its
name, or the following groups of them:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It \fB:all\fP
All the functions and constants.
.It \fB:register\fP
The \fBregister_*\fP and \fBunregister_*\fP functions.
.It \fB:log\fP
The log functions and the \fBLOG_*\fP constants.
.It \fB:metric\fP
The \fBMETRIC_*\fP constants.
.It \fB:notif\fP
The \fBNOTIF_*\fP constants.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Any Perl module will start similar to:
.Bd -literal
    package Load;

    use strict;
    use warnings;

    use NCollectd qw(:all);
.Ed
.Pp
A very simple read function might look like:
.Bd -literal
    sub load_read
    {
        open(my $fh, "<", "/proc/loadavg") or die "Cannot open file: $!";
        my @load = split(/\es+/, <$fh>);
        close($fh);

        my $fam = NCollectd::MetricFamily->new("system_load", METRIC_GAUGE,
                                               "System load averages");
        $fam->append(NCollectd::MetricGaugeDouble->new($load[0], {duration => "1m"}));
        $fam->append(NCollectd::MetricGaugeDouble->new($load[1], {duration => "5m"}));
        $fam->append(NCollectd::MetricGaugeDouble->new($load[2], {duration => "15m"}));
        $fam->dispatch();
    }
.Ed
.Pp
A very simple write function might look like:
.Bd -literal
    sub gauge_write
    {
        my ($fam) = @_;

        return if $fam->type != METRIC_GAUGE;

        print "# TYPE ", $fam->name, " gauge\en";
        print "# HELP ", $fam->name, " ", $fam->help, "\en" if defined $fam->help;
        for my $metric (@{$fam->metrics}) {
            my $labels = $metric->labels;
            my $set = join(",", map { "$_=\e"$labels->{$_}\e"" } sort keys %$labels);
            printf("%s{%s} %.15g %d\en", $fam->name, $set, $metric->value,
                   $metric->time * 1000);
        }
    }
.Ed
.Pp
To register those functions with ncollectd:
.Bd -literal
    register_read(\e&load_read);
    register_write(\e&gauge_write);

    1;
.Ed
.Pp
See the section \fBCLASSES\fP above for a complete documentation of the data
types used by the read and write functions.
.Sh CAVEATS
ncollectd is heavily multi-threaded, but there is only one Perl interpreter:
like the Python interpreter, it can only run the callbacks of one thread at a
time, and a callback that takes a long time delays the callbacks of the other
threads.
The data is shared between all the callbacks, there is no need to use the
\f(CWthreads::shared\fP module, and the \f(CWthreads\fP module must not be used.
.Pp
Do not call \f(CWexit\fP from a callback, it ends the ncollectd process.
Use \f(CWdie\fP to report errors.
.Pp
Each module is usually only loaded once and kept in memory for performance
reasons.
Therefore, END blocks are only executed once when ncollectd shuts down.
You should not rely on END blocks anyway - use \fBshutdown functions\fP
instead.
.Pp
The perl plugin exports the internal API of ncollectd which is considered
unstable and subject to change at any time.
We try hard to not break backwards compatibility in the Perl API during
the life cycle of one major release.
However, this cannot be guaranteed at all times.
Watch out for warnings dispatched by the perl plugin after upgrades.
.Sh "SEE ALSO"
.Xr ncollectd 1 ,
.Xr perl 1 ,
.Xr ncollectd-python 5 ,
.Xr ncollectd.conf 5
